refactor: filter via the URL, and stop shipping JS for it

The filter was client state, which meant 24 KB of gzip for four choices that are
really four pages. `?status=overdue` is shareable, survives reload, works with the
Back button, and renders with JavaScript off. The page is a server component
again, so the toggle library and both its files are gone.

parseFilter matches the incoming value against the known list instead of casting
it. A visitor can type anything into that parameter; `?status=<script>` now
behaves exactly like no parameter, and a test asserts it.

Also added: a Vietnamese not-found page. With real URLs in play, a mistyped path
was landing on Next's default English 404 with no way back.

Client JS: 638 KB raw / 196 KB gzip -> 580 KB / 176 KB. Verified with the
detector at 1280 and 390, by clicking through at both widths, by navigating
?status= directly, and with JavaScript disabled.

Tests 36 to 37.
